<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tichitt culture</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tichitt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tichitt culture was a prehistoric culture of stone-built settlements in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>, centred on the escarpments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhar-tichitt">Dhar Tichitt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhar-walata">Dhar Walata</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> trace the origins of Ghana, or Wagadou, to this culture, which it dates archaeologically to around 1600 BCE. It is regarded as one of the earliest examples of complex settlement in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tichitt-culture.jpg" alt="Tichitt culture" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sylvie Amblard-Pison · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Haches_et_herminettes_provenant_de_villages_du_Dhar_Tichitt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
